# Edgeberry Device Hub — Mosquitto ACLs
# Ref: https://mosquitto.org/man/mosquitto-conf-5.html#acl_file
# Notes:
# - For PROD (mTLS), set `use_subject_as_username true` and the cert CN becomes the username.
# - `%u` expands to the authenticated username - but with `use_subject_as_username`, that is
#   the client cert's full rendered Subject DN, which for these single-field "/CN=<uuid>" certs
#   is the literal string "CN=<uuid>" (verified: `openssl x509 -noout -subject` on an issued cert
#   prints `subject=CN=<uuid>`, and mosquitto's own connection log shows the same). Every topic in
#   this system is built from the bare UUID, so a pattern built from %u can never match one -
#   confirmed live: every device's twin/get and twin/update publish was being denied, silently,
#   since %u never equals the topic's UUID segment. Do not reintroduce %u below without also
#   stripping the "CN=" prefix somehow (mosquitto's ACL patterns have no substring support).
# - `%c` expands to the bare client id, which devices set to their UUID - used below instead.
#   Trade-off: MQTT client id is client-chosen and not cryptographically bound to the cert the
#   way the Subject DN is, so a device with a *valid* cert for its own UUID could still pick a
#   different client id and attempt to write to another device's twin topics. Bounded by two
#   things for now: exploiting it requires an already-whitelisted, already-issued certificate
#   (see ENFORCE_WHITELIST), and claiming an in-use client id forces mosquitto to disconnect
#   whichever session already holds it, which is immediately visible. Tightening this properly
#   needs a real auth plugin that can read the cert CN itself rather than relying on the ACL
#   file's %u/%c macros - out of scope for this MVP fix.

## mTLS-only: usernames come from client cert CNs
## No password-based users required.

# --- Device identity (mTLS) ---------------------------------------------------
# Devices are identified by their certificate CN (= deviceId)
# Grant each device read/write access to its own topic namespace
# MVP: Avoid broad access to non-namespaced device topics
#pattern write devices/%u/#
#pattern read  devices/%u/#


# Provisioning (do NOT rely on CN - the provisioning cert is shared fleet-wide,
# so its CN doesn't identify a single device). Authorize by MQTT clientId
# instead: the provisioning connection sets clientId = UUID, so %c scopes each
# device to its own claim/cert exchange. Previously this used the + wildcard,
# which let any holder of the (fleet-shared) provisioning cert subscribe to
# every device's provision/accepted topic and observe - or race to spoof a
# response to - another device's claim, including its issued certificate.
pattern write $devicehub/devices/%c/provision/request
pattern read  $devicehub/devices/%c/provision/accepted
pattern read  $devicehub/devices/%c/provision/rejected

# Twin flow (device twin interactions)
# Devices write get/update; read accepted/rejected + delta
pattern write $devicehub/devices/%c/twin/get
pattern write $devicehub/devices/%c/twin/update
pattern read  $devicehub/devices/%c/twin/update/accepted
pattern read  $devicehub/devices/%c/twin/update/rejected
pattern read  $devicehub/devices/%c/twin/update/delta

# Device heartbeat (devices need to publish heartbeat messages)
pattern write $devicehub/devices/+/heartbeat

# Telemetry and events (devices publish sensor data and events)
pattern write $devicehub/devices/+/telemetry
pattern write $devicehub/devices/+/events/+
pattern write $devicehub/devices/+/status

# Direct methods (devices need to subscribe to method calls and respond)
pattern read  $devicehub/devices/+/methods/+/request
pattern write $devicehub/devices/+/methods/+/response

# Cloud-to-device messages (devices subscribe to messages from cloud applications)
pattern read  $devicehub/devices/+/messages/devicebound

# Optionally allow devices to read public broadcast announcements (if any)
# topic read $devicehub/broadcast/#

# Note: Backend services connect via the local anonymous listener (127.0.0.1:1883)
# without TLS in MVP. No service-specific ACLs are defined here. If services move
# to mTLS on 8883 later, define per-service users (CN) and permissions accordingly.
